<exercises xml:id="ex-IntegralTest">
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What are the conditions of the Integral Test? What are the conclusions of the Integral Test?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-IntegralTestProblems">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                For each of the following series, apply the Integral Test and interpret the conclusion of the test.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^\infty \left(\frac{k}{k^2+1}\right)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\sum_{k=1}^\infty \left(\frac{k}{k^2+1}\right) \amp  \sim \int_{x=1}^\infty \frac{x}{x^2+1}\;dx </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \frac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+1)\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=t}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \frac{\ln(t^2+1)}{2} - \frac{\ln(2)}{2}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \infty</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The integral diverges, and so the series must diverge as well.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^\infty \left(\frac{k}{e^k}\right)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\sum_{k=1}^\infty \left(\frac{k}{e^k}\right) \amp \sim \int_{x=1}^\infty xe^{-x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \left(-xe^{-x} - e^{-x}\right)\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=t}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp =\lim_{t\to\infty} -\frac{t}{e^t} - \frac{1}{e^t} + \frac{1}{e} + \frac{1}{e}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{2}{e} </mrow>
                    </md>
                    The integral converges, and so the series must converge as well.    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=2}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{k\ln(k)} \right)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\sum_{k=2}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{k\ln(k)} \amp \sim \int_{x=2}^\infty \frac{1}{x\ln(x)}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \ln(\ln(x))\bigg|_{x=2}^{x=t}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \ln(\ln(t)) - \ln(\ln(2))</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \infty</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The integral diverges and so the series diverges as well.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^\infty \left( \frac{k^2+k}{e^k} \right)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\sum_{k=1}^\infty \left( \frac{k^2+k}{e^k} \right) \amp \sim \int_{x=1}^{\infty} (x^2+x)e^{-x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty}\left(-\frac{x^2}{e^x} - \frac{3x}{e^x} - \frac{3}{e^x}\right)\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=t}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} - \frac{t^2}{e^t} - \frac{3t}{e^t} - \frac{3}{e^t} + \frac{1}{e} + \frac{3}{e} + \frac{3}{e}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{7}{e}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The integral converges, and so the series converges as well.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{k^2+1} \right)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\sum_{k=1}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{k^2+1} \right) \amp \sim \int_{x=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{x^2+1}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \tan^{-1}(x)\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=t}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \tan^{-1}(t) - \tan^{-1}(1)</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{\pi}{2} - \frac{\pi}{4}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{\pi}{4}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The integral converges and so the series converges as well.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-pSeriesConverge">
        <statement>
            <p>
                For what values of <m>p</m> (with <m>p\gt 0</m>) will the following series converge?
                <md>
                    \sum_{k=1}^\infty \frac{1}{k^p}
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-GeometricConverge">
        <statement>
            <p>
                For what values of <m>r</m> (with <m>r\gt 0</m>) will the following series converge?
                <md>
                    \sum_{k=0}^\infty r^k
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</exercises>